values ('98f08500-0000-5a12-967f-5b555027b772', 'ddad1e11-32c5-529e-9c8e-937caed5d76b', $nm$Hoverla
from Zaroslyak$nm$, $nm$CARPATHIANS, UKRAINE$nm$, $nm$https://smdnbjvelqsgjoubognc.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/route-covers/curated/hoverla-zaroslyak.jpg$nm$, 'moderate', 'foot', $nm$FOOT$nm$, 3.77, $nm$48.1643° N, 24.5358° E$nm$, null, $nm$2.5 H$nm$,
  738, 2061, ARRAY[0.0,0.005,0.009,0.017,0.023,0.034,0.048,0.067,0.097,0.121,0.15,0.167,0.181,0.187,0.195,0.213,0.233,0.258,0.275,0.287,0.287,0.302,0.316,0.348,0.378,0.43,0.489,0.556,0.603,0.634,0.653,0.68,0.712,0.738,0.77,0.809,0.868,0.933,0.975,1.0]::numeric[], $nm$2061 M$nm$, $nm$The short way up Ukraine's highest peak. A marked footpath leaves the Zaroslyak trailhead at about 1,290 m and climbs 740 m in 3.8 km — roughly a 20% average gradient — over Mala Hoverla (1,762 m) to the 2,061 m summit. OpenStreetMap rates the path as plain hiking (SAC T1), with no technical sections, but the steepness makes it a real mountain day: allow two to three hours up. Two springs and two camp spots are mapped within 150 m of the track.

Route data © OpenStreetMap contributors (ODbL). OSM relation/10330801.
